{
  "lessons": [
    {
      "canDo": "Explain why Bell-test violations rule out local hidden instructions",
      "description": "Use a simple Bell game to understand the local limit, experimental violations, and what those violations do, and do not, prove.",
      "estimatedMinutes": 5,
      "screens": [
        {
          "activityTemplate": null,
          "kind": "hook",
          "visual": "A source sends one particle toward station A and another toward distant station B; each station has an independently adjustable measurement device and a result display, with no connection between the stations.",
          "visualKind": "picture",
          "brief": "Two distant lab stations record results that agree more often than any shared instruction sheet seems to allow. How can researchers test whether the particles brought their answers with them?",
          "skills": []
        },
        {
          "activityTemplate": null,
          "kind": "explanation",
          "visual": null,
          "visualKind": "table",
          "brief": "Introduce a Bell game: two stations independently receive random inputs, 0 or 1, and return bits without exchanging messages; shared instructions are allowed. Show the winning rule in a table: inputs 00, 01, and 10 require matching outputs; 11 requires different outputs.",
          "skills": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "activityTemplate": null,
          "kind": "guess",
          "visual": null,
          "visualKind": "none",
          "brief": "Ask: “With equally likely input pairs, what is the best long-run winning rate that shared instructions can achieve?” Options: “75%,” “About 85%,” and “100%”; answer: 75%, with immediate feedback that no instruction sheet can satisfy all four requirements.",
          "skills": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "activityTemplate": null,
          "kind": "explanation",
          "visual": null,
          "visualKind": "none",
          "brief": "A local hidden-variable model lets shared information determine each output using only that station’s input. Write one instruction sheet as a₀, a₁ for station A and b₀, b₁ for B: the subscript names the input.",
          "skills": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "activityTemplate": null,
          "kind": "workedExample",
          "visual": null,
          "visualKind": "none",
          "brief": "Work through the contradiction: winning 00, 01, and 10 requires a₀=b₀, a₀=b₁, a₁=b₀, hence a₁=b₁. But winning 11 requires a₁≠b₁: every sheet loses at least one equally likely pair, so its winning rate is at most 3/4.",
          "skills": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "activityTemplate": null,
          "kind": "check",
          "visual": null,
          "visualKind": "none",
          "brief": "Give the sheet a₀=0, a₁=1, b₀=0, b₁=0 and ask which input pair loses, then its winning rate. Answer: 10 loses, giving 75%; include the tempting mistake that 11 loses because different outputs are always bad.",
          "skills": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "activityTemplate": null,
          "kind": "explanation",
          "visual": null,
          "visualKind": "none",
          "brief": "Randomly switching instruction sheets cannot raise an average above their individual 75% ceiling. This experimentally testable limit is a Bell inequality; it also covers local models whose outputs use randomness.",
          "skills": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "activityTemplate": null,
          "kind": "explanation",
          "visual": null,
          "visualKind": "none",
          "brief": "An entangled pair has a joint quantum state that cannot be written as a separate state for each qubit. For example, a pair can give matching 0-or-1 results when measured alike without having a shared classical answer sheet.",
          "skills": [
            1
          ]
        },
        {
          "activityTemplate": null,
          "kind": "explanation",
          "visual": null,
          "visualKind": "none",
          "brief": "With suitable measurements on an entangled pair, quantum theory predicts a Bell-game winning rate of cos²(π/8) ≈ 85.36%, above 75%. Experiments observe violations of the local limit, not merely matching outcomes.",
          "skills": [
            1
          ]
        },
        {
          "activityTemplate": null,
          "kind": "check",
          "visual": null,
          "visualKind": "none",
          "brief": "Ask whether stations that always return 0, producing perfectly matching outputs, have demonstrated a Bell violation. Answer: no—they win only 75% of this game; include “Yes, because their outputs always agree” as the tempting error.",
          "skills": [
            0,
            1
          ]
        },
        {
          "activityTemplate": null,
          "kind": "explanation",
          "visual": "A single experimental layout with a central pair source and stations A and B separated by 1.2 km; label the station-to-station light-travel time approximately 4 μs and each station’s choice-to-output interval within the same 1 μs window.",
          "visualKind": "picture",
          "brief": "To exclude ordinary communication, each station must choose its input and finish its output before light could carry the other station’s choice across. This is spacelike separation: across 1.2 km, light needs about 4 μs, so completing both sides within 1 μs can meet this condition.",
          "skills": [
            1
          ]
        },
        {
          "activityTemplate": null,
          "kind": "explanation",
          "visual": null,
          "visualKind": "none",
          "brief": "Discarding missed detections can select a misleadingly successful sample. In this game, every declared trial must count, with a preset output for a missed detection rather than silently dropping that trial.",
          "skills": [
            1
          ]
        },
        {
          "activityTemplate": null,
          "kind": "explanation",
          "visual": null,
          "visualKind": "none",
          "brief": "The 75% limit concerns a long-run average, so a short run can exceed it by chance. Experiments need a statistical analysis that shows how strongly their results exclude the local limit, not just a percentage above it.",
          "skills": [
            1
          ]
        },
        {
          "activityTemplate": null,
          "kind": "check",
          "visual": null,
          "visualKind": "table",
          "brief": "Compare two otherwise well-controlled runs using valid statistical intervals: run 1 has an estimated winning rate of 82%, interval 80–84%; run 2 has 82%, interval 72–92%. Ask which excludes 75% at the stated confidence level: only run 1; include “Both, because both estimates exceed 75%.”",
          "skills": [
            1
          ]
        },
        {
          "activityTemplate": null,
          "kind": "explanation",
          "visual": null,
          "visualKind": "none",
          "brief": "A Bell violation does not provide faster-than-light messaging: each station’s individual results remain random, so its partner cannot control a readable message. The conclusion excludes local hidden-variable explanations with setting choices independent of the hidden variables—not every possible hidden-variable theory.",
          "skills": [
            1
          ]
        },
        {
          "activityTemplate": null,
          "kind": "application",
          "visual": null,
          "visualKind": "none",
          "brief": "A lab reports an 83% winning rate with a valid interval of 81–85%, independently chosen settings, spacelike-separated measurements, and every declared trial counted. Ask you to replace “Particles sent messages faster than light” with a defensible headline and identify two experimental safeguards supporting it; the headline should reject local hidden instructions under the stated assumptions.",
          "skills": [
            0,
            1
          ]
        }
      ],
      "skills": [
        {
          "description": "Show why instructions that use only each station’s own input cannot win more than three-quarters of the Bell game.",
          "example": "The requirements a₀=b₀, a₀=b₁, and a₁=b₀ force a₁=b₁, contradicting the fourth winning requirement.",
          "hard": true,
          "name": "Explain the local limit in Bell tests",
          "topic": "Bell inequalities",
          "useCase": "Understand why shared randomness alone cannot reproduce the correlations tested in a quantum experiment."
        },
        {
          "description": "Explain how a statistically supported Bell violation excludes local hidden-variable models under the experiment’s assumptions without enabling faster-than-light messages.",
          "example": "A well-controlled winning rate of 83%, with a valid interval of 81–85%, excludes the 75% local limit.",
          "hard": false,
          "name": "Explain what Bell tests showed",
          "topic": "Bell-test interpretation",
          "useCase": "Evaluate claims about quantum experiments without confusing nonclassical correlations with faster-than-light communication."
        }
      ],
      "title": "Bell's test"
    }
  ]
}

